DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/german-studies-lecture-series
 -jutta-eming-how-do-you-dance-a-cabinet-of-curiosities\n\nPlease join Germ
 an Studies Lecture Series for How Do You Dance a Cabinet of Curiosities? A
  conversation with Professor Jutta Eming (Freie Universität Berlin).\n\nA
 s a collection and exhibition convention\, a Cabinet of Curiosity or Wunde
 rkammer is regarded as a historical intermediate stage between the medieva
 l treasury and the modern museum. It was widespread throughout Europe from
  the 16th to 18th century until its holdings began to be distributed to ne
 wly founded natural history\, art\, or ethnological museums in the 18th an
 d 19th century. Taking a recent choreography as a starting point\, this ta
 lk introduces the view that the Wunderkammer is not primarily an early mod
 ern form of collection\, but a cultural-historical phenomenon that spans e
 pochs and is part of a continuum of discourses on the marvelous. This incl
 udes the fact that the Wunderkammer has obviously become a broader cultura
 l studies topic and forms a reference for a variety of recent academic and
  non-academic trends. In premodern times these cabinets constitute a Europ
 ean view of the world with curiosity in “monstrous” races on its margi
 ns\, and a practice of perception in which narration\, appropriation\, col
 lecting\, and presentation are closely linked. In recent times this intere
 st in curiosity has flipped in emancipatory ways: The Wunderkammer now see
 ms to provide a space in which humans negotiate the limits of binary ident
 ity and redefine their place in the world.\n\nRSVP for the Jutta Eming eve
 nt here.
